/// The lock screen's password check: a PAM helper of its own
/// (`strand-auth`, a separate process) checks each password with the
/// `strand` PAM service, or `login` when `/etc/pam.d/strand` is missing.
/// Only a success unlocks the session lock; anything else leaves it
/// locked. While a lock is shown PAM's conversation runs, so a module
/// that needs no password (a fingerprint reader) can unlock it, and PAM's
/// texts and prompts show as PAM says them.
service auth {
  /// A password is being checked: PAM has it, or it waits for PAM to ask
  /// (typed while a fingerprint reader waits).
  busy: bool
  /// The last password was refused, or could not be checked.
  failed: bool
  /// What PAM last told the user, as it said it ("Place your finger on
  /// the fingerprint reader", "The account is locked"); "" once PAM next
  /// asks for a password, and while no lock is shown. Show it as it is:
  /// `if auth.message != "" { text auth.message }`.
  message: text
  /// The hidden prompt PAM waits on ("Password: "), or "" while it waits
  /// on none (a fingerprint reader, a password being checked).
  prompt: text
  /// Checks `password`: `input { type: password; text: <-> secret;
  /// focus: true; on activate { if secret != "" { auth.submit(secret) };
  /// secret = "" } }` (Return in the field activates it; an empty field
  /// is not sent, since with pam_faillock each empty Return pressed to
  /// wake the screen would count as a failed login). Every password,
  /// empty or not, goes to PAM: it answers the prompt PAM waits on, or
  /// the next one it asks. Ignored while one is being checked.
  action submit(password: text)
}
